{
  "id": "COMPOUND:18987 - PROTEIN:1642",
  "verbalization": "Bonducellpin E is shown to have a target relationship with the Gamma-aminobutyric acid receptor subunit gamma-2, which in turn is targeted by Ac-Tyr-Gly-Asn-Thr(1)-Met-Lys(2)-Tyr-Pro-Ser-Asp(1)-Trp-Glu(OMe)-Glu(2)-Tyr-OH and Ugonin G, both of which target Proto-oncogene tyrosine-protein kinase Src. This suggests that Bonducellpin E may exert its biological effect through a pathway involving the Gamma-aminobutyric acid receptor subunit gamma-2, which is a known target of multiple compounds. The presence of multiple targets for the Gamma-aminobutyric acid receptor subunit gamma-2, including Ac-Tyr-Gly-Asn-Thr(1)-Met-Lys(2)-Tyr-Pro-Ser-Asp(1)-Trp-Glu(OMe)-Glu(2)-Tyr-OH and Ugonin G, indicates that the receptor may be a key mediator in the signaling cascade initiated by Bonducellpin E. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Bonducellpin E may act through a mechanism involving the Gamma-aminobutyric acid receptor subunit gamma-2, potentially modulating the activity of Src, a proto-oncogene tyrosine-protein kinase. The evidence suggests a plausible mechanistic link, but further validation through experimental studies is required to confirm the direct interaction and the specific role of the receptor in Src activation.",
